---
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { site } from '../config';
import { url } from '../lib/url';

const tech = url('technical/#enterprise');
const techProofs = url('technical/#proofs');
const techLimits = url('technical/#limits');

const faqs: { q: string; a: string; tbc?: boolean }[] = [
  {
    q: 'Does it work everywhere?',
    a: `
      <p>Only where networks are registered. Everywhere else your phone shows <strong>Unverified</strong>: nothing to compare with, which is exactly where you are today. Unverified is not a warning.</p>
      <p hidden>The more operators register, the more networks can show <strong>Verified</strong>. A <strong>Conflict</strong> needs no wide adoption at all: one registered network is enough for its impostors to be flagged.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a WiFi network need to take part?',
    a: `
      <p>Two things, both done once by the operator:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Register the network.</strong> The operator proves to a Geo CA that it runs the network at this place and registers its name, security type and, if it has one, its sign-in page domains. The result is a GeoCert in the public GECKO map.</li>
        <li><strong>Let phones reach the GECKO map before sign-in.</strong> Networks with a sign-in page already let a few addresses through before you log in, such as the sign-in page itself. The GECKO map joins that list, so the phone can check the network before you type anything.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Enterprise networks such as eduroam should also accept GECKOGuard's shared onboarding login and give it a walled garden that reaches only GECKO. Universities publish a realm record, so their students are protected on other campuses too. <a href="${tech}">How that works, step by step</a>.</p>
      <p>Networks that don't take part keep working as before. Phones show them as <strong>Unverified</strong>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Who decides who may register a place?',
    a: `
      <p>A <strong>Geo CA</strong>: an authority that checks an operator really runs a network at an address before issuing its GeoCert, much like a certificate authority checks who owns a website. Organisations that already verify addresses could fill the role, such as a postal service or a business-listing provider like Google Business.</p>
      <p hidden>Two rules keep it fair. A registration names a verified registrant (a domain such as <code>airport-wipkingen.com</code>) and a network name in an area belongs to one operator: a second operator can't register the same name on top.</p>
      <p>This part is the proposed design and still an open question.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Who runs the GECKO map and why trust it?',
    a: `
      <p>You don't have to trust whoever runs it, because your phone checks every answer instead. The map keeps all registrations in a tamper-evident structure, like a Certificate Transparency log and signs its current state. Each answer comes with a proof that it matches that signed state and is cryptographically complete.</p>
      <p hidden>So the map can't hand your phone a registration that isn't in it, leave one out without the proof failing, or quietly rewrite its history. If something doesn't check out, the result is <strong>Conflict</strong>, never <strong>Verified</strong>.</p>
      <p hidden>GECKO comes from the Network Security Group at ETH Zürich. The prototype runs its own map in the lab. <a href="${techProofs}">How the proofs work</a>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is my location sent anywhere?',
    a: `
      <p>Only to the GECKO map and only roughly. To ask what is registered nearby, GECKOGuard sends the map cells covering an area around you (about ${site.lookupRadiusMeters}&nbsp;m in the prototype) and an altitude range. That is not an exact position and nothing else about you or your device is part of the request.</p>
      <p>The map server can still tell roughly where the request came from, so who runs it matters for privacy. That is listed with the other <a href="${techLimits}">open points</a>.</p>`,
    tbc: !site.lookupAnswerConfirmed,
  },
  {
    q: 'Why not just use eduroam CAT?',
    a: `
      <p><strong>eduroam CAT</strong> (Configuration Assistant Tool) is how universities hand out safe eduroam settings today. You download your university's profile from cat.eduroam.org or the geteduroam app and it installs the login server's name and certificate authority on your device. With that profile, your phone rejects an impostor's server. That is exactly the protection GECKOGuard sets up too.</p>
      <p>The catch is getting the profile:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>It needs internet first, so you have to set it up before you arrive, over another network.</li>
        <li>Everyone has to know about it and do it. Many people just tap eduroam and accept the certificate prompt.</li>
        <li>It only covers eduroam and only institutions that publish a profile. Company networks rely on their own device management for the same thing.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>GECKOGuard ends up with the same kind of pinned profile, but gets it during the first connection, through the onboarding walled garden and checks it through GECKO. There is no separate step to remember and like CAT it only happens once per account. <a href="${tech}">The eduroam example</a> shows it step by step.</p>`,
  },
];
---
<section class="section" id="faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
  <div class="container faq__grid">
    <header class="faq__head">
      <h2 class="display-2" id="faq-title">Questions and Answers</h2>
      <p class="lead">Additional information about GECKOGuard</p>
      <!-- <p><a href={url('technical/')}>For the curious: how it works in detail</a></p> -->
    </header>
    <div class="accordion">
      {faqs.map((f, i) => (
        <div class="accordion__item">
          <h3>
            <button class="accordion__trigger" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls={`faq-${i}`} id={`faq-${i}-t`}>
              {f.q}
              <Icon name="chevron" />
            </button>
          </h3>
          <div class="accordion__panel" id={`faq-${i}`} role="region" aria-labelledby={`faq-${i}-t`} hidden>
            <div class="faq__answer" set:html={f.a} />
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<script>
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('#faq .accordion__trigger').forEach((t) => {
    t.addEventListener('click', () => {
      const open = t.getAttribute('aria-expanded') !== 'true';
      t.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
      const panel = document.getElementById(t.getAttribute('aria-controls')!);
      if (panel) panel.hidden = !open;
    });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .faq__grid { display: grid; gap: var(--space-10); }
  .faq__head { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); align-content: start; }
  .faq__tbc { margin-top: var(--space-3); }
  .faq__answer { display: grid; gap: var(--space-3); }
  .faq__answer :global(ul) { display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); padding-inline-start: var(--space-5); }
  .faq__answer :global(strong) { color: var(--color-text-primary); font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold); }
  @media (min-width: 56em) {
    .faq__grid { grid-template-columns: 5fr 7fr; gap: var(--space-16); }
    .faq__head { position: sticky; top: calc(var(--layout-nav-height) + var(--space-8)); }
  }
</style>
